<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uta von Ballenstedt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1000–1046</span> · Also known as Uta of Naumburg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Uta von <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ballenstedt">Ballenstedt</a> was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> noblewoman, the wife of Margrave Ekkehard II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a>. Her statue by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naumburg-master">Naumburg Master</a> in the west choir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naumburg-cathedral">Naumburg Cathedral</a>, carved about two centuries after her death, became one of the most celebrated figures of medieval German art.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/uta-von-ballenstedt.jpg" alt="Uta von Ballenstedt" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Linsengericht · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Naumburg-Uta.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
